  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The Ghana Essential Health Intervention Programme (GEHIP)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">In Ghana, levels of sickness and death among women and children under five remain unacceptably high. 450 per 100,000 women die every year, compared with 12 per 100,000 in the UK. Almost 1 in 10 children in Ghana die before their fifth birthday, mainly from diseases which are preventable or could be treated if care was given in time. In 1994 the &#x201c;Navrongo Experiment&#x201d; in Northern Ghana halved child deaths in the area in three years by posting community nurses to provide &#x201c;doorstep services&#x201d; in rural communities. This Community Health Planning and Services programme placed a focus on preventing ill health and treating common illnesses. Although Ghana built this approach into its national strategies in 2000, the pace of scale up has been slow, due to weaknesses in the health system. This project will build on an existing programme, helping to develop a sustainable ambulance service which is suited to local conditions, and adapting the Community Health Planning and Services model to suit urban areas.</narrative>
  </description>
